phy: m31: dphy-tx0: bound the PLL-lock wait with a timeout

reset() busy-waited on the DPHY PLL lock with a bare `while(!is_pll_locked());`.
If the PLL is never programmed - e.g. sys_m31_dphy_tx_configure() was called
with a bitrate that matches no entry in the m31 table (notably 0), so it writes
no PLL registers - this spins forever, hard-hanging the CPU. It bites at the
DRM fbdev initial modeset, which runs under console_lock, so the hang is silent
(no printk reaches the console). Bound the wait (~100 ms) and log an error so a
misconfiguration is a recoverable modeset failure instead of a dead board.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [vendor-kernel hardening]

--- a/drivers/phy/m31/phy-m31-dphy-tx0.c	2026-07-14 21:27:38.581683429 +0100
+++ b/drivers/phy/m31/phy-m31-dphy-tx0.c	2026-07-14 21:27:38.604069828 +0100
@@ -210,8 +210,17 @@
 	dev_info(dphy->dev, "2 SET_U0_MIPITX_DPHY_RESETB\n");
 
     if (!assert) {
-		while(!is_pll_locked(dphy));
-		dev_info(dphy->dev, "MIPI dphy-tx # PLL Locked\n");
+		int timeout = 100000;
+
+		/* Bound the PLL-lock wait: an unprogrammed PLL (e.g. an unknown
+		 * bitrate that matched no table entry) would otherwise busy-wait
+		 * here forever and hard-hang the CPU under console_lock. */
+		while (!is_pll_locked(dphy) && --timeout)
+			udelay(1);
+		if (timeout <= 0)
+			dev_err(dphy->dev, "MIPI dphy-tx # PLL lock TIMEOUT\n");
+		else
+			dev_info(dphy->dev, "MIPI dphy-tx # PLL Locked\n");
     }
 }
 
